course: dictionary
lesson: 12032
title: A word for the Christian world
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a whole group of people and places. «Christendom» is one of them. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it: they are its context, and they leave clues. «The knight was the bravest in all Christendom.» The words «in all» tell you the word covers a very large area, so Christendom must mean a big group of places.
explain: The dictionary says Christendom means all the Christian people and Christian countries of the world, thought of together. In the Middle Ages, many people in Europe followed the Church, so they thought of their lands as one Christian group. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Christendom is a noun, and it has no plural, so you do not say "Christendoms". The ending -dom often shows a realm or state, as in kingdom. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Christendom means all the Christian people and countries of the world, thought of together.
fact: In the Middle Ages, Christendom often meant the parts of the world where Christianity was the main religion, especially Europe.
fact: In history lessons, «Christendom» was often led by kings and the Church.
fact: In old stories, «the finest in all Christendom» means the finest anywhere.
fact: «Christendom» is an uncountable noun, so it has no plural.
exercise: «The knight was the bravest in all Christendom.» What does Christendom mean here? | Only in one small village | A type of horse | *Anywhere in the Christian world | Under the sea
exercise: Ella says Christendom is just a fancy word for one church building. What is closer to the truth? | A single building where people pray | The name of a king's castle | *All the Christian people and countries of the world, thought of together | A kind of song
exercise: Find and copy the word that names the Christian world in this sentence: «The knight was the bravest in all Christendom.» | bravest | the | *Christendom | knight
test: «Christendom» is a noun. Which sentence about it is correct? | Christendom is a colour | Christendom is a verb meaning to pray | *Christendom is a noun for a group of people and lands | Christendom is a number
test: A history book says that in the Middle Ages Christendom was led by kings and the Church. Who led it? | *Kings and the Church | Children from every school | Farmers and sailors | Pirates
exam: Which sentence uses Christendom correctly? | We ate Christendom for lunch. | Christendom ran across the field. | *The old story praised the finest knight in all Christendom. | Christendom is my favourite colour.
